  "worked_solution": "According to the superposition principle, if the function ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and $c$ is a constant, then $y(x)=c{y}_{1}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)={e}^{(x-3)/3}={e}^{-1}\\cdot {e}^{x/3}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. In this case, $c={e}^{-1}.$\n\n- The function $y(x)=\\frac{x}{3}{e}^{x/3}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the product of a non-constant function and a known solution.\n\n- The function $y(x)=\\frac{5{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x/3}}=5\\cdot {e}^{x-2x/3}=5{e}^{x/3}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. In this case, $c=5.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"",
